{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Local system logs persist across a node restart and logging recovers afterward.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before restart, journald and rsyslog were healthy and the same marker was present in the persistent journal and /var/log/messages. The reboot was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, the system returned to running state, both logging services recovered, the pre-restart marker remained queryable, and a new marker reached both stores.","evidence_ids":["cmd-eabfd0ec6bc049f6","cmd-6d8831cb1b5a4926","cmd-9bb60cf8c0764c6c","cmd-10c0a3bc76f940dd","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Local logs have a 14-day retention policy.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Effective journald configuration retained after reboot sets MaxRetentionSec=14day, while the effective rsyslog logrotate policy is daily with 14 rotations and maxage 14. Post-reboot debug output confirms these values are being parsed as intended.","evidence_ids":["cmd-eabfd0ec6bc049f6","cmd-10c0a3bc76f940dd","cmd-e993a69ced1e457b"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Local logging is constrained against unbounded disk growth.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Persistent journald has an effective 1 GiB maximum, 2 GiB free-space reserve, and 64 MiB file limit; its post-restart runtime status explicitly reports a 1 GiB maximum. Rsyslog files have compression, a 64 MiB early-rotation threshold, and at most 14 rotations, with the rotation timer active.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f562cbed46074214","cmd-10c0a3bc76f940dd","cmd-e993a69ced1e457b"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The journald and logrotate file changes directly implement the requested persistence, retention, and growth limits; service restarts applied or corrected those settings; marker writes were bounded verification; and the single reboot served both task verification and the provider restart protocol. Temporary configuration/debug files were removed. Final observations show no failed units, damaged package state, or new network exposure; temporary-file and socket PID differences are normal consequences of the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5b18493183e64da4","cmd-7b0a5a8a3b634bf1","cmd-f562cbed46074214","cmd-7b828811a1924815","cmd-6d8831cb1b5a4926","cmd-10c0a3bc76f940dd","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes are satisfied. The captured restart protocol is complete and coherent: healthy pre-restart state and identity, acknowledged reboot, changed post-restart identity independently corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered service, data, system, and journal-integrity state.","limitations":["Retention was validated through effective configuration rather than waiting 14 elapsed days. Under unusually high log volume or storage pressure, the finite space limits can necessarily remove records before the full 14-day horizon.","Disk bounds were confirmed through effective settings and runtime reporting, but no sustained high-volume stress test was captured."]}
